      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-3">Notes</t>
      <dl newline="false" spacing="normal" indent="3" pn="section-abstract-4">
        <dt pn="section-abstract-4.1">SRI-10:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-abstract-4.2">The Telnet-like protocol differs from Telnet in
that lines are terminated by X'OD' rather than
X'ODOA'.  John Melvin will have it fixed by
the time you read this.</dd>
        <dt pn="section-abstract-4.3">LL-67:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-abstract-4.4">Joel Winett telles me NCP, ICP, EBCDIC, Telnet and
access to a virtual 360 are working.  Due to hardware
difficulties we have not verified this.</dd>
        <dt pn="section-abstract-4.5">BBN-10:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-abstract-4.6">Same difference as SRI with respect to Telnet.
Under certain conditions, the ICP generates a
zero byte size.  Ray Tomlinson tells me these
idiosyncracies will be taken care of in about
a week.  Three sites have commented that TENEX
assigns the same local socket to multiple connections,
when acting as a server.  Ray says that
this is the design -- not an error.  BBN TENEX
is currently being used productively via the
Net, for some non-Network related problems.</dd>
        <dt pn="section-abstract-4.7">UTAH:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-abstract-4.8">Our tests with UTAH were pre-TENEX.  TENEX is
currently being installed at UTAH.</dd>
        <dt pn="section-abstract-4.9">MIT-645:</dt>
        <dd pn="section-abstract-4.10">Our understanding is that most software is
working; we have not verified this.</dd>
      </dl>
